  "summary": "A distinctive aesthetic characterized by the microgrooved surface of vinyl records and their colorful labels.",
  "description": "Features a smooth, black surface with concentric circular grooves and colorful paper labels; developed as a more durable and higher-fidelity alternative to shellac records in the 1940s. The aesthetic encompasses both the physical object and its cultural significance in music history and collecting.",
  "culturalContext": "Vinyl record surface — the microgroove lattice and its colored-light play — turned mass-produced media into fetish texture: rainbow reflections across LP grooves, colored and picture-disc geometry, splatter pressings, and the ritual-of-play dust-crackle. Record fairs and Discogs collecting culture treat pressings as objects of study (\"audiophile 180g\", first-pressing matrices); the groove's spiral is now a universal graphic metaphor for sound itself.",
